---
title: One Source, Many Channels
type: pattern
created: 2026-04-13
updated: 2026-04-13
sources:
  - raw/conversations/ncme-2026-ai-native-dissertation.qmd
  - raw/references/dissertation-framework-readme.md
  - FOUNDATIONS/application/platform-integration.md
  - FOUNDATIONS/architecture/democratic-design.md
tags:
  - architecture
  - multi-channel
  - distribution
  - accessibility
  - democratic-access
---

Author content once in a domain-native language (R + Quarto), and the framework automatically produces all distribution channels: interactive website, formal PDF, working paper, REST API, MCP tools, and interactive web application.

## Problem/Context

Researchers and domain experts currently maintain separate artifacts for different audiences: a PDF for the committee, a slide deck for conferences, a dashboard for stakeholders, a data dump for collaborators. Each artifact is authored independently, creating maintenance burden, version drift, and accessibility gaps. Non-technical audiences often get the least investment — a static PDF instead of an interactive exploration tool.

## Solution

A unified authoring environment (R package + Quarto) that produces multiple output channels from the same source files:

| Channel | Audience | Technology | Access |
|---|---|---|---|
| Interactive website | General readers | Quarto HTML | Browser |
| Thesis/report PDF | Institutional requirements | Quarto PDF | Print/reader |
| Working paper | Peer reviewers | Quarto HTML+PDF | Versioned |
| REST API | Data analysts | RestRserve/Plumber | HTTP |
| MCP tools | AI agents | JSON schema | Programmatic |
| Web application | Non-specialists | NextJS + React | Interactive dashboard |

All channels read from the same R functions and Quarto content. When the analysis logic changes, every channel updates. When new data arrives, every channel reflects it.

## Implementation Details

The multi-channel pipeline is orchestrated by `ai()` in dataimago-rpkg:

- **Quarto profiles** handle document variants: `_quarto.yml` (website), `_quarto-thesis.yml` (PDF), `_quarto-working-paper.yml` (HTML+PDF). Same `.qmd` source files, different rendering profiles.
- **R API layer** wraps exported functions as HTTP endpoints. `run_dissertation_api()` starts the server; each endpoint maps 1:1 to an R function.
- **MCP schema** generated from R function signatures. AI agents discover tools via the schema without prior knowledge of the API.
- **NextJS app** consumes the API (live in dev, static JSON in production) and renders interactive components: data explorer, chapter navigation, visualizations.
- **Static export** runs all R functions and writes JSON to `public/api/`, enabling serverless deployment.

The build pipeline: `quarto render` (all profiles) + `pnpm build` (NextJS) + `export_static_api()` (JSON) = complete distribution.

## Ethical Justification

Multi-channel distribution is an expression of democratic participation. When knowledge exists only as a PDF, access is limited to those who can read academic prose. An interactive dashboard serves non-specialists. An API serves data analysts. MCP tools serve AI agents that can synthesize findings across many sources. Each channel is a different form of access — and access is justice.

This pattern also prevents the common failure mode where "the real results" live in a researcher's R console while stakeholders get a simplified (often misleading) summary. Every audience sees the same underlying data and analysis.

